  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/adec7c5c587dcaf899d53432f724a52c0b59d40dfc9d5fafc6cfca92ca3e052e.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?",
  "worked_solution": "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=4,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ but they are not jump discontinuities.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=-1$ only.",
